  "query_preview": "..A dry cold front has pushed southward across our \nregion this afternoon, shifting winds to north/northeast and \ndropping dewpoints into single digits. Strongest winds have been \nnortheast of the Mogollon Rim with gusts 25-35 mph, though speeds \nhave been decreasing. For tonight, clear skies and the very dry air \nmass in place will lead to efficient radiational cooling in areas \nthat aren't impacted by winds. In areas that experience light winds \ntonight, this could lead to some of the coldest temperatures in a \nfew weeks. This includes most areas north of the Mogollon Rim. Teens \nare in the current forecast Saturday morning at Tusayan, Bellemont, \nand Window Rock - the typical cold spots. Gusty northeast winds will \ncontinue Saturday morning along/south of the Mogollon Rim, decreasing\nlater in the afternoon. \n\nSaturday through Monday - weak high pressure will bring a warming \ntrend in daytime temperatures, reaching 5-10 degrees above normal by \nSunday/Monday. Some mid-level moisture moving in from the southwest \nSunday afternoon into Monday should increase cloud cover, with a low \nchance of a shower Sunday evening and again Monday afternoon mainly \nnear the White Mountains.\n\nTuesday through Thursday - low pressure along the west coast will \nbring increasing southwest winds Tuesday, followed by cooler temperatures\nWed/Thu as it moves eastward into our region. Most ensemble members\nkeep the low too far north for precipitation in our area at this time,\nso PoPs are less than 10% for now.",
